  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A stork landed in Germany with a spear through it. The bird was alive, nesting, behaving as if nothing had happened.",
        "visual_query": "white stork nest germany",
        "visual_fallback": "stork nest"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Mecklenburg, 1822. The shaft ran two and a half feet long, and no hunter in Europe carried a weapon anything like it.",
        "visual_query": "baltic coast german countryside",
        "visual_fallback": "coastal farmland"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So where had the bird been? Every winter Europe's storks vanished. Naturalists argued they hibernated underwater, or changed into other creatures entirely.",
        "visual_query": "european marsh winter reeds",
        "visual_fallback": "frozen reeds"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The spear was identified as central African. That meant the stork had been speared there, survived it, and flown thousands of miles home.",
        "visual_query": "central african savanna river",
        "visual_fallback": "african savanna"
      },
      {
        "narration": "One bird proved migration. It is still in Rostock's collection, stuffed, spear included, and roughly two dozen more have turned up since.",
        "visual_query": "white storks flying migration",
        "visual_fallback": "birds flying"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
